Title: Agents poll with sleep while waiting for background jobs: verify what is in place, then close the gap
Created: 2026-10-25
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-10-25T06:26Z claude created
2026-10-25T06:28Z reviewer claude note Evidence from arena leg 1372-f1m (cycle 4, 2026-10-24), Pi session read call by call: 2 668 s of sleep in 11 waits. Two were pgrep waiters of the 0889 shape and ran to their iteration cap: 'for i in 1..20; pgrep -f "dvc pull"; sleep 15' lasted 301 s although dvc pull had finished within 30 s, and 'for i in 1..30; pgrep -f check1372.sh; sleep 20' lasted 601 s although the third gate run (wall 272 s) was long done: the waiting shell matches its own command line, so the loop never sees the job end. That is 902 s, 34% of the sleep. The other nine waits were fixed sleeps (5 to 420 s) set without knowing the job length; e.g. sleep 420 on a 276 s gate run. Nothing ended the long waits except the loop cap or the timer.

--- body ---
## Context

Agents wait for background jobs by looping on `sleep`, and the time is large. Measured on the arena (ticket 1094,
cycle 4, 2026-10-24, derived from Pi session timestamps by `scripts/leg-timing.py`): leg `1372-f1m` took 3 787 s of which
2 668 s were `sleep` (70 %); `1372-f1x` took 1 833 s with 1 126 s of `sleep`. The earlier handoff recorded `sleep` as 7.8 % of local
leg wall-clock. These are polling loops on background test runs, not the model's speed.

## What was found before writing this (to be confirmed in the first step)

- Ticket 0889 (2026-09-10) added a PreToolUse guard against shell loops that poll `pgrep` for a process: 105 such waiters over
  551 transcripts, two stranded processes. The guard `scripts/guard-pgrep-waiter.sh` no longer exists: commit 90a62683
  (ticket 0976) deleted eight PreToolUse guards "that never earned their place". It covered `pgrep` loops, not `sleep` polling on a log.
- The portable-wait design of 2026-10-21 (journal `2026-10-21-await-external-probes.md`; PRs #1542, #1552; ticket 1163; the
  stopguard design, ticket 1164, deferred) chose the instruction route first: each runtime's own background facility with a deadline.
- The snapshot pinned for the arena night (`night-pins/2026-10-24-c4-r1/payload`, the Pi agent directory) contains no wait or
  sleep rule that I could find by grepping `pgrep`, `sleep` and `background job`.

## First step

Verify, before deciding anything: does any rule or skill that reaches Pi tell the agent how to wait for a background job; what
do the long legs actually run (`sleep N` in a loop, on a log or on a process); which runtime facility could replace it
(a blocking run with a timeout, a notification when the job ends); and was the instruction route implemented for Pi or only
for Claude Code. Record the answer in the ticket log.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The finding above, confirmed or corrected, with the commands used
- [ ] A decision: close as already addressed (with the evidence), or the smallest change that reaches the Pi legs
- [ ] If a change is made, a re-measure of `sleep` share on a few legs after the change
